course: dictionary
lesson: 7926
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word read has more than one meaning, and the sentence around it tells you which one. To read is to look at written words and understand what they say. «Nobody in the family can read or write, and they do not mind.» Here read means being able to understand written words.
explain: Read can also mean to say written words out loud for others, or to understand what a clock, a map or a thermometer shows. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then choose the meaning that fits. Then try it in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: «Reading your work aloud helps you hear missing words, clumsy sentences and tense slips.» Here reading aloud means saying the words out loud, so you can catch mistakes your eyes might miss. The word aloud is the clue.
explain: «Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath.» Here read means look carefully and understand what the story really says. A good reader does not stop at the headline.
fact: Read is a verb: read, reads, read, reading.
fact: To read is to look at written words and understand what they say.
fact: To read aloud is to say the words out loud for others to hear.
fact: To read a clock, a map or a thermometer is to understand what it shows.
fact: A read is a time spent reading, as in «a good read».
exercise: Ms Patel says, «Read the question again: what does it ask for?» What does read mean here? | *To look at the words carefully and understand them | To count the pages | To say the words out loud to the class | To run to the front of the room
exercise: «Reading your work aloud helps you hear missing words.» What does reading aloud mean? | Writing the words on the board | Looking at the words in silence | Skipping over the hard words | *Saying the words out loud
exercise: Sam says, «That was a good read.» What does read mean here? | A part of a clock | *A time spent reading a book | A map of the town | A loud noise
test: Ravi reads the thermometer and says it is 20 degrees. What does read mean here? | To break it open | *To understand what the thermometer shows | To paint it a new colour | To sing a song about it
test: «Headlines are written to grab you, so read the story underneath.» What does read mean here? | To skip the story completely | To copy the headline onto paper | To shout the headline out loud | *To look carefully and understand what the story really says
exam: «Nobody in the family can read or write.» In this sentence, what does read mean? | To say words out loud for others | *To understand written words | To tell the time from a clock | A good read
